      "image": "videos/09-chess-master-memory/2.jpg",
      "seconds": 24.79,
      "text": "Chase and Simon ran this in nineteen seventy three. Shown real positions from real games, masters recalled them far better than novices. Then the researchers scattered the pieces at random. The master's advantage collapsed almost entirely. They had never been memorizing twenty five pieces. They were memorizing five patterns, each one a chunk they had seen ten thousand times before.",
